
<p>Robbie Whelan, Anissa Gardizy, Kate Clark, reporting for The Wall Street Journal:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>In his final months as chief executive of Nvidia, Jensen Huang
was laying the groundwork to pull back the company’s approach to
backstopping the AI economy.</p>

<p>Now, with a finance chief at the helm who was already wary of some
deals the ousted chief forged, the startups who had been counting
on Nvidia’s support expect an even greater pullback.</p>

<p>Just last month, the CEOs of emerging cloud companies that buy
Nvidia chips and rent out computing power gathered in Berlin for
Nvidia’s premier European artificial-intelligence conference. In a
talk that surprised some, Huang’s message was clear: Nvidia’s days
as an open checkbook were over.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>This makes sense. But how does this jibe with Nvidia moving forward on closing their preposterous $13 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, which <a href="https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-to-acquire-hugging-face/">Jensen Huang personally announced on September 3</a>? That acquisition epitomizes Nvidia’s role as the “open checkbook” for the entire AI industry. In August, just before the acquisition was announced, <a href="https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-hugging-face-annualized-revenue-jumps-50-150-million/">The Information reported</a> that Hugging Face’s annual revenue was just $150 million. Typical SaaS companies have valuations around 10× annual revenue. Let’s say Hugging Face is so super duper special that they justify 20×. That’s $3 billion. (I do not think Hugging Face is super duper special.) $13 billion is closer to 100×, which is stupid.</p>

<p>If the Huang era is over, Nvidia should unwind this deal.</p>

